implementationPlan.description:
MODEL AND SUPERVISOR: keep a live daemon's daemon.json when its recorded bind does not serve 127.0.0.1. Load test-driven-development: write BH1-BH3 first and watch BH1 fail at an assertion (today's code deletes the file and spawns), then implement. Edit only with your own editor (Edit/Write on Claude, apply_patch on codex exec), never a shell write or a subagent edit (global rail). First read mem:decision-daemon-cli-bindhost-local-rule, mem:gotcha-jetbrains-read-daemon-info-deletes and mem:gotcha-kotlin-test-names-no-dot.

1. Models.kt, data class DaemonInfo: append a fifth parameter after projectPath:
   // The daemon records it only for a --host or MOE_BIND_HOST other than 127.0.0.1 (packages/moe-daemon/src/index.ts startDaemon).
   val bindHost: String? = null
   It is trailing and defaulted, so every positional 4-argument DaemonInfo(...) in main and test code still compiles. Gson leaves it null when daemon.json has none, and omits a null one when the tests write daemon.json, so existing fixture bytes are unchanged. deleteDaemonInfoIfUnchanged's data-class equality now also covers bindHost; that is intended.

2. MoeDaemonSupervisor.kt companion, right after WSL_BIND_HOST:
   // The binds that serve 127.0.0.1, the only address this plugin dials: the daemon's probeHostFor
   // (packages/moe-daemon/src/server/probeHost.ts) probes each at 127.0.0.1. The daemon never records 127.0.0.1 itself.
   private val LOOPBACK_SERVING_BIND_HOSTS = setOf("127.0.0.1", "0.0.0.0", "::", WSL_BIND_HOST)
   Exact, case-sensitive matches, as the daemon compares. Not the whole 127.0.0.0/8: a daemon bound to 127.0.0.2 does not answer on 127.0.0.1. Not "localhost": Node may bind it to ::1.

3. A public method next to isReachable, with a KDoc that says what the bullets below say:
   fun offLoopbackBindRefusal(info: DaemonInfo): String? = try {
       val host = info.bindHost
       when {
           host.isNullOrEmpty() || host in LOOPBACK_SERVING_BIND_HOSTS -> null
           info.pid <= 0 || isOtherSideOfWsl(info.projectPath, isWindows()) || !isProcessAlive(info.pid) -> null
           else -> "The Moe daemon for this project (pid ${info.pid}) listens on $host, but this IDE connects on 127.0.0.1 only, " +
               "so Moe kept .moe/daemon.json and started no second daemon. Stop that daemon, or restart it without --host " +
               "or MOE_BIND_HOST, then use Restart Daemon."
       }
   } catch (ex: Exception) {
       log.debug("Failed to check the daemon.json bindHost: ${ex.message}")
       null
   }
   - Keep the condition order: the string checks, then pid <= 0 (no daemon has it; on Windows pid 0 is the idle process), then the other-side check, and the pid probe last. An other-side pid lives in another namespace and is never probed (the existing isReachable rule), so such a record goes on as before.
   - This error handling mirrors isReachable: any failure means "go on as before".
   - Never dial, resolve or probe bindHost. daemon.json is repository content (the task-d6ebe4ab boundary), and the plugin dials ws://127.0.0.1 only, so a probe could not be used anyway.
   - The text starts with fixed words, never with the host, so a Swing label never renders repository text as HTML.

4. ensureDaemonRunningImpl: insert this between the reachable block (if (info != null && sameProject == true && isReachable(info)) { ... }) and the stale cleanup (if (info != null && sameProject != null && deleteDaemonInfoIfUnchanged(info))):
   // A live daemon bound off 127.0.0.1 is out of reach, not stale: keep its daemon.json and start no second daemon.
   val bindRefusal = if (info != null && sameProject == true) offLoopbackBindRefusal(info) else null
   if (bindRefusal != null) {
       log.warn(bindRefusal)
       onStatus(false, bindRefusal)
       return false
   }
   It applies to the same project only: another project's record is still removed as today. After a PROTECTED Stop and Replace the old pid is dead, so the guard lets the spawn go on.

5. MoeDaemonSupervisorTest.kt: add three tests after `ensureDaemonRunning leaves an open other-side daemon of this project alone`, reusing supervisor(...), initializedRoot, writeDaemonInfo, otherSideSpelling and FakeProcess. Name folders by row index, because a ':' in a folder name fails on Windows. No '.' or ':' in any test name, so never an IP literal.
   - `BH1 a live daemon bound off loopback keeps its daemon json and starts no second daemon` (DoD 1). For (index, host) in listOf("::1", "192.0.2.10", "localhost", "127.0.0.2").withIndex(): root = initializedRoot("off-loopback-$index"); file = writeDaemonInfo(root, DaemonInfo(9876, 42, "now", root.canonicalPath, bindHost = host)); before = file.readBytes(). Build a supervisor whose onStatus records (connected, message), with portProbe = { _, _ -> false }, processAliveProbe = { it == 42 } and processStarter = { starts++; FakeProcess() }. With host as the assertion message: ensureDaemonRunning() is false; starts is 0; assertArrayEquals(before, file.readBytes()); exactly one status was published, connected false, and its message contains host and "127.0.0.1". Do not assert an exact probe list: isReachable probes pid 42 before the guard does.
   - `BH2 a dead or non-positive pid, or a bind that serves loopback, is replaced as today`. Rows (bindHost, pid, alive): (null, 42, true), ("", 42, true), ("wsl", 42, true), ("0.0.0.0", 42, true), ("::", 42, true), ("127.0.0.1", 42, true), ("::1", 42, false), ("::1", 0, true). Each row uses initializedRoot("replaced-$index"), portProbe false and processAliveProbe = { alive }. Assert ensureDaemonRunning() is true, there was exactly one start, the file is gone and no status was published. Call killSpawnedDaemon() after each row.
   - `BH3 an other-side record bound off loopback never has its pid probed`. For open in listOf(true, false): DaemonInfo(9876, 42, "now", otherSideSpelling(root.canonicalPath), bindHost = "::1"), processAliveProbe = { probed += it; true }, portProbe = { _, _ -> open }. Assert as the existing other-side test does: ensureDaemonRunning() == !open; probed is empty; open keeps the bytes with 0 starts; closed removes the file with 1 start. Call killSpawnedDaemon() after each row.
   Do not edit any existing test: they must pass unchanged (DoD 2).

